WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.900 --> 00:00:07.610
Germans love their boardgames and an absolute
classic is "Fang den Hut" or "Catch the hat."

00:00:07.610 --> 00:00:11.410
Teams of little hats move around the board
trying to catch one other –

00:00:11.410 --> 00:00:14.009
or the special golden hat.

00:00:14.009 --> 00:00:19.170
But today we're putting a political twist
on this family favorite and playing "Catch

00:00:19.170 --> 00:00:20.640
the voter."

00:00:20.640 --> 00:00:27.789
Here are our parties, raring to go, ready
for the race to parliament.

00:00:27.789 --> 00:00:32.110
They stretch from the far left of the political
spectrum to the far right, and they're all

00:00:32.110 --> 00:00:35.280
trying to get our voter to support them.

00:00:35.280 --> 00:00:43.010
But who is this voter, and what do they want
from the parties?

00:00:43.010 --> 00:00:47.370
All German citizens over the age of 18 can
vote in federal elections.

00:00:47.370 --> 00:00:52.860
That's a total of 60.4 million people, just
over half of whom are women.

00:00:52.860 --> 00:00:57.120
It's a pretty multicultural group, with almost
a third having at least one parent who was

00:00:57.120 --> 00:00:59.390
born in another country.

00:00:59.390 --> 00:01:02.320
One of the most interesting aspects is age.

00:01:02.320 --> 00:01:06.180
Less than 15% of the voting population are
younger than 30.

00:01:06.180 --> 00:01:11.360
Nearly 40% are over 60, and half of those
are over 70.

00:01:11.360 --> 00:01:13.860
It also matters where the voters are.

00:01:13.860 --> 00:01:17.710
Germany's cities and regions have strong local
identities.

00:01:17.710 --> 00:01:22.120
Their priorities are affected by things like
the local economy, infrastructure and even

00:01:22.120 --> 00:01:26.280
religion.

00:01:26.280 --> 00:01:29.810
Some of the issues on voters' minds are very
much signs of the times.

00:01:29.810 --> 00:01:35.540
No prizes in this game for guessing that the
COVID-19 pandemic is right up there.

00:01:35.540 --> 00:01:39.740
In Germany, the regional state authorities
have a lot of decision-making power in areas

00:01:39.740 --> 00:01:41.260
like health and education.

00:01:41.260 --> 00:01:45.970
During the pandemic, this led to a patchwork
of different rules across the country.

00:01:45.970 --> 00:01:50.170
But what do voters want to see done at the
federal level?

00:01:50.170 --> 00:01:57.350
The next government should dedicate itself
to problems that have been highlighted by

00:01:57.350 --> 00:01:58.700
the pandemic, such as faltering digitalization.

00:01:58.700 --> 00:02:01.960
First is, ensure full immunization of the
complete population in Germany.

00:02:01.960 --> 00:02:03.340
Second is no more lockdowns.

00:02:03.340 --> 00:02:07.350
I want my future government to restore my
freedom.

00:02:07.350 --> 00:02:08.619
We have to be prepared.

00:02:08.619 --> 00:02:14.590
If there's a mutation, we have to be prepared
to answer to that as quickly as possible.

00:02:14.590 --> 00:02:19.000
Next up, climate change – a topic that's
difficult to ignore after the deadly floods

00:02:19.000 --> 00:02:20.650
in Germany this summer.

00:02:20.650 --> 00:02:25.749
In a recent survey more than 80% of participants
said they want to see urgent action on climate

00:02:25.749 --> 00:02:29.739
protection – a view shared across generational
and party lines.

00:02:29.739 --> 00:02:35.980
Natural catastrophes were always really far
away, but at the moment they're right on our

00:02:35.980 --> 00:02:36.980
doorstep.

00:02:36.980 --> 00:02:39.239
It feels like the whole world is burning right
now.

00:02:39.239 --> 00:02:44.959
I think we need an earlier coal phase-out
and instead invest in more renewable energy.

00:02:44.959 --> 00:02:50.180
I hope the next government interferes more
in our daily lives, so that we understand

00:02:50.180 --> 00:02:52.779
that things can't go on like this.

00:02:52.779 --> 00:02:59.689
You need to propose solutions for all the
cuts you're going to make to social lives

00:02:59.689 --> 00:03:01.180
and industry.

00:03:01.180 --> 00:03:05.739
Immigration &amp; foreign policy have of course
been huge topics in Germany in recent years,

00:03:05.739 --> 00:03:08.989
and they're back in focus following recent
developments in Afghanistan.

00:03:08.989 --> 00:03:13.919
This is one area that polarizes voters, and
it has pushed some away from the larger centrist

00:03:13.919 --> 00:03:14.919
parties.

00:03:14.919 --> 00:03:19.150
When it comes to refugees, Germany clearly
needs more regulation, checks and, if necessary,

00:03:19.150 --> 00:03:20.150
deportations.

00:03:20.150 --> 00:03:25.120
We need workers and these people need somewhere
to live.

00:03:25.120 --> 00:03:33.319
Especially if you consider that Germany played
a role in the reason why many of these people

00:03:33.319 --> 00:03:36.250
had to flee in the first place.

00:03:36.250 --> 00:03:42.859
I also think that the EU needs to find more
solutions for sharing the load fairly between

00:03:42.859 --> 00:03:44.700
the countries.

00:03:44.700 --> 00:03:50.169
Issues which always feature heavily include
education, the economy and jobs, security,

00:03:50.169 --> 00:03:55.879
and the distant dream of digitalization.

00:03:55.879 --> 00:03:58.120
And remember all those over-60s voters?

00:03:58.120 --> 00:04:03.059
Their voting power is clear in the prominence
of "Rentenpolitik" or pension policy in the

00:04:03.059 --> 00:04:07.959
party manifestos.

00:04:07.959 --> 00:04:12.599
Germany hasn't yet jumped on the worldwide
trend towards personality-driven politics.

00:04:12.599 --> 00:04:17.909
Once again in this year's line-up of chancellor
candidates, there's no sign of a larger-than-life

00:04:17.909 --> 00:04:20.759
maverick, or celebrity-turned-politician.

00:04:20.759 --> 00:04:24.340
But in any case, voters here don't directly
elect the Chancellor.

00:04:24.340 --> 00:04:28.430
They vote for members of parliament – and
they're the ones who'll decide who gets Angela

00:04:28.430 --> 00:04:30.110
Merkel's old job.

00:04:30.110 --> 00:04:35.430
Each voter does, however, have two votes – one
for a local candidate and one for a party

00:04:35.430 --> 00:04:37.300
on the national level.

00:04:37.300 --> 00:04:41.669
This means they could vote tactically for
two different parties, focusing on local issues

00:04:41.669 --> 00:04:45.639
with their first vote and ideological interest
with the second.

00:04:45.639 --> 00:04:50.789
In the last election in 2017, a quarter of
voters split their ballots in this way.

00:04:50.789 --> 00:04:55.580
While TV is still the main source of information
for most Germans, the switch to digital platforms

00:04:55.580 --> 00:05:00.479
has begun, with smaller parties, especially
on the right, having made the most ground

00:05:00.479 --> 00:05:01.479
so far.

00:05:01.479 --> 00:05:05.509
Some of the larger parties are trying to catch
up on Facebook, hoping to bag themselves some

00:05:05.509 --> 00:05:06.509
boomers.

00:05:06.509 --> 00:05:10.800
But they've also begun to make a play for
younger voters on platforms like TikTok – with

00:05:10.800 --> 00:05:13.610
varying degrees of success and plenty of cringe.

00:05:13.610 --> 00:05:17.789
So, on September 26th it's time to roll the
dice again.

00:05:17.789 --> 00:05:22.000
Which groups will have the biggest turnout
– men or women, young or old?

00:05:22.000 --> 00:05:25.200
People in the east or west, or people in the
cities or in the countryside?

00:05:25.200 --> 00:05:29.800
And what will going through their minds in
the voting booth – will it be migration?

00:05:29.800 --> 00:05:30.800
Jobs?

00:05:30.800 --> 00:05:31.800
Climate?

00:05:31.800 --> 00:05:34.259
Or simply the faces of the top candidates?

00:05:34.259 --> 00:05:39.000
It's all to play for in the 2021 finale of
"Catch the voter."

